# English summaries of the 100 entries of 《俱舍英譯百瑕》 (editor's condensation of the author's Chinese).
# ZH_OVERRIDE: hand-picked Chinese gist where automatic extraction of the author's first sentence is unsuitable.
# CONCEPT: link each entry to a concept id in the atlas (optional).

EN = {
1: "The nine non-appropriated elements include the whole dharma element (法界全), not 'one half' or 'one part' of it; Xuanzang's 全 is explicit, and the Prakaraṇa confirms it.",
2: "Slip: 'defiled' should be 'undefiled' — the verse speaks of the undefiled (不染). LS is correct here.",
3: "Both translations say mental displeasure (憂根) is abandoned only by cultivation; it should be the pain faculty (苦根). LS's prose is correct.",
4: "'Or [the] second' is wrong: at this point one can enter only the fundamental first dhyāna, not the fundamental second dhyāna.",
5: "Both translations (and Paramārtha) narrow the acquisition of cessation abandoned by cultivation to 'not obtained by noble ones'. Xuanzang's 'not drawn forth by the noble path' (非聖道所引) is complete: noble ones also obtain a with-outflow acquisition of cessation.",
6: "ājñā (已知根, the faculty of knowing) should be ājñātāvīndriya (具知根, the faculty of one who has fully known). Paramārtha's 知已根 corresponds to Xuanzang's 具知根.",
7: "LS slip: 'have been abandoned' should be 'have not been abandoned' (未斷). Pruden is correct.",
8: "LS twice has 'obscured-non-defined' where the text means unobscured-indeterminate (無覆無記); also a numbering slip (4-6 for 4-5). Pruden is correct.",
9: "Two errors: the trainee's mind becoming manifest is not just the receptivity to the dharma-knowledge of suffering (that covers only the first of four cases); and 'immaterial realm' at the end is wrong.",
10: "The flaw goes back to the Sanskrit and Paramārtha: rendering 自境 'own object' as 'own realm' is unsuitable, since immaterial-realm mental approaches can take the outflow-free path of the subsequent-knowledge class as object.",
11: "A being of a lower stage possesses only defiled dharmas of a higher stage because it is not yet detached from the lower stage, not because it is born below; the parenthetical gloss misexplains this.",
12: "A partial negation is turned into a total one: it should be 'not all joy etc. are mental approaches'.",
13: "'At the same time' is wrong: 俱 says that the world's varieties all arise from action, not that they arise simultaneously.",
14: "Noble ones do not possess only 'pure' non-informative matter: born in the sensuous or fine-material realm they have both dhyāna and outflow-free restraint, and an embryo necessarily has the impure dhyāna restraint. Nor is relinquished informative action only that produced by indeterminate volition.",
15: "'Only' qualifies the lay follower's restraint, not sexual misconduct: only the restraint of lay followers is [limited to abstaining from] sexual misconduct…",
16: "Pruden renders 相續 (saṃtati) 'some persons'; it means a being's continuum over time — the same person before and after becoming someone's spouse.",
17: "LS adds '[with the exception of the restraint of the fast]' needlessly: only the one-day restraint is relinquished by the fifth cause, the others by the first four.",
18: "Both omit the sensuous realm: one born there may also possess outflow-free restraint. Sanskrit, Paramārtha and Xuanzang all include it.",
19: "LVP sees two readings of the verse (retribution of intermediate-dhyāna action; retribution occurring in the intermediate dhyāna). There is only one: the retribution of intermediate-dhyāna action, as Paramārtha's prose and the Sanskrit show.",
20: "'Definitely bringing retribution' and 'when detaching permanently from the stage' describe one action, not two; such action is actually of the type 'to be experienced at an indeterminate time'.",
21: "The fourth kind of action ('neither black nor white…') is only part of outflow-free action; the translations suggest all outflow-free action belongs to it, and that only outflow-free action destroys the other three.",
22: "Pruden's 'in addition to hell' is wrong: black-white action is experienced in the sensuous realm other than hell. LS is right.",
23: "An essay on how the five classes of anuśaya are defined (Sanskrit, Paramārtha, Xuanzang, Saṃghabhadra's critique). The translations make the abandoned thing the anuśaya's object ('when its object can be abandoned by seeing a truth'); but what is abandoned is the anuśaya itself, which takes as object what is abandoned by seeing that truth (若緣見此所斷為境). This misreading is not in Vasubandhu's text.",
24: "LS 'subsequent cognition' should be the receptivity to subsequent knowledge (類智忍). Pruden is right.",
25: "Noble ones do not abandon defilements only with outflow-free knowledge; they may also use with-outflow knowledge (Xuanzang's 如其所應). Xuanzang also precisely distinguishes 害 from 斷.",
26: "LS's 'afflicted views' should be 'views' (views are not necessarily defiled), and 'five aggregates' should be the five appropriative aggregates (五取蘊).",
27: "LS should not insert '[Answer by the Vaibhāṣikas:]': what follows is Vasubandhu citing the Sarvāstivāda treatise to show it conflicts with the preceding argument.",
28: "The logic is misread: the whole sentence is Vasubandhu's objection, not a possible Vaibhāṣika answer.",
29: "Taking the sentence 'according to kind' is acceptable, but 'not according to kind' (preferred by the Vibhāṣā and Nyāyānusāra) is better and more complete.",
30: "Not 'none will arise' but 'not all will arise': some will and some will not.",
31: "It should be 'all [anuśayas] of the fine-material realm', not 'the universal ones of the fine-material realm'.",
32: "The translations' third and fourth groups should be one: the 'universal' ones here are the universal anuśayas of the immaterial realm.",
33: "'Undefiled [minds], in which [anuśayas] adhere-and-grow only [as object]' are minds not associated with anuśaya; both take them as associated.",
34: "Double negation lost: 'not active' should be 'not inactive' (非不隨增 = they do adhere-and-grow), and the subject is the dharmas associated with view, not persons.",
35: "'Badly done' misleads: remorse can arise about good or bad deeds; remorse over unwholesome deeds is wholesome, remorse over wholesome deeds unwholesome. As an enveloper it is defiled and its object is good.",
36: "LS's '[thirty-eight]' and its calculation in n. 497 are wrong: false view, clinging to views etc. abandoned by seeing suffering include some with their own realm as object and must be counted.",
37: "The numbering '4, 5, 6, 8, 9' should be '2, 4, 6, 8, 9'. Introduced by an essay on which thorough knowledges are relinquished and obtained.",
38: "One may say the only fruit of the boundary of the immaterial is the knowledge of the destruction of greed for the fine-material, but not the converse.",
39: "Slip: the fifth moment is the receptivity to the dharma-knowledge of origin.",
40: "The parenthetical gloss is wrong: 'free from both bonds' means free from bonds of one's own and other classes and grades, and applies to the dharmas of all five classes, not only those abandoned by seeing.",
41: "On obtaining only the seventh thorough knowledge: two conditions are required (having the seventh, not the eighth), and the cases are misstated.",
42: "The translations omit the case of relinquishing only the eighth (see the essay with entry 37).",
43: "Less accurate than Xuanzang: they omit 'and when that person attains arhatship'.",
44: "One who enters the path of seeing after detaching from most of the sensuous realm is not a 'gradual' practitioner, yet falls under this case; Xuanzang dropped 次第 to avoid the confusion.",
45: "'Sixth and seventh' should be 'seventh and eighth'.",
46: "Higher receptivity need not have the aspect 'impermanent': it contemplates sensuous-realm suffering with any one of its four aspects.",
47: "LS's perfect tense ('have been abandoned by insight') is wrong: receptivity precedes the path of seeing; the point is that these defilements will never arise from now on.",
48: "LS slip: should be 'not to arise'. Pruden is right.",
49: "'After' reverses Xuanzang: pratyekabuddhas other than the rhinoceros-like may switch vehicle at the stages of heat or summit, i.e. before receptivity, not after them.",
50: "A small flaw, very common: the eighth knowledge of direct realization, subsequent knowledge of the path, is already the path of cultivation. Direct realization is not the same as the path of seeing; only its first fifteen moments are.",
51: "The gloss of the 'path of a superior fruit' (勝果道) as aiming to abandon defilements not yet abandoned is inaccurate.",
52: "'By means of the path of seeing' is wrong: such a noble one gains the outflow-free pleasure faculty in this life through the path of a superior fruit, not the path of seeing; the notes' logic is also wrong.",
53: "LS's 'only' is wrong: 能越一處 means passing beyond at least one place, not only one. Pruden is suitable.",
54: "One does not become a non-returner after being born in the fine-material realm: the fruit of non-returning is obtained only in a sensuous-realm body.",
55: "Not only non-returners: any noble one who will pass through a rebirth in the sensuous realm has no refinement of faculties or regression.",
56: "The diamond-like concentration can be produced on nine stages, with the dharma-knowledge of cessation or path or any of the four subsequent knowledges.",
57: "It is the defilements of a stage that cannot be counteracted by the worldly path of that same stage, not the path that 'is opposed'.",
58: "There are two acquisitions, one with-outflow (worldly) and one outflow-free (supramundane), not one acquisition that is both.",
59: "The logic of the verse on the summit of existence is mistranslated: it shows that one can lack a defilement without possessing the corresponding acquisition of disconnection.",
60: "Only the Buddha removes the traces (習氣) and undefiled ignorance, not the unshakable arhat.",
61: "Relinquishing the previously obtained path includes both the path of the fruit and the path of the candidate; both translations mention only the latter.",
62: "Faculties (two: dull, sharp) are confused with lineages (six). Each change of lineage needs one uninterrupted and one liberation path, so changing from the dull lineage 'liable to fall' to sharp takes five pairs.",
63: "The trainee's refinement of faculties is not only about the fruit of once-returning; the fruits of stream-entry and non-returning are also relinquished.",
64: "There is no fruit of non-returning belonging to the immaterial realm; the translations, and their rendering of Paramārtha, imply that there is.",
65: "The added explanation of 同 (same object, aspect, outflow-free) is problematic: the diamond-like concentration and the knowledge of destruction may differ in aspect and object.",
66: "Right speech and right action are not one group with right livelihood another: right speech plus part of right livelihood is vocal action; right action plus the other part is bodily action.",
67: "It should be 'dharma-receptivity' and 'dharma-knowledge', but both are rendered 'dharma-knowledge'. LS is right here.",
68: "LS misreads 'them' as paths of liberation and advancement; it refers to the defilements. Pruden is fine.",
69: "Two errors in the notes (from LVP) about acquisitions of disconnection on the path of seeing; the verse cited concerns noble ones on the path of cultivation.",
70: "'And' should be 'or': these are two knowledges (dharma-knowledge of cessation, of path), not one knowledge cognizing both.",
71: "'Always' and 'must be added' are wrong: the dharmas following a wholesome mind amount at most to four aggregates.",
72: "'Another person' is unnecessary (an influence of LVP via Puguang): a with-outflow mind may be cognized by greed of one's own past or future too.",
73: "The notes praise Paramārtha's rendering, yet the translations' prose follows the other reading.",
74: "Xuanzang is clear and accurate here: knowledge of others' minds cognizes neither the acquisition of greed nor a mind 'cognizing a mind with greed', so 'bound by greed' cannot be called 'with greed'.",
75: "Adding 'with mind' to 'associated / dissociated' is superfluous and misleading: mind itself is an associated dharma, but is not associated with mind.",
76: "The 'associated dharmas' of conventional knowledge include mind as well as mental factors, and the co-existent dharmas include those of mental factors.",
77: "Not only the sensuous realm: knowledge that takes everything as object belongs to the sensuous and fine-material realms.",
78: "The notes (from LVP citing Yaśomitra) seriously misread Vasubandhu's logic about general conventional knowledge being born only of hearing and reflection.",
79: "LS's 'and from cultivation' is wrong: Xuanzang's Kośa has 'only born of hearing and reflection'; LS conflates the Kośa with the Xianzong lun (顯宗論) cited afterwards.",
80: "A badly wrong reading caused by LVP's punctuation; Saṃghabhadra is in fact refuting the Kośa's 'otherwise one would detach all at once'.",
81: "'A knowledge of the mind of another' should be conventional knowledge (世俗智).",
82: "'[Respectively]' should not be added.",
83: "'The other noble ones' (disciples) include those beyond training, not only trainees.",
84: "Drop 'eight': only seven of the eight liberations are shared with ordinary beings.",
85: "'Exist in' is inaccurate: universal defilements take the whole realm and stage as object; they do not 'exist' in it.",
86: "'The first three' is wrong in the translations' ordering of the powers (which follows Paramārtha); Xuanzang's first three are magic, divine eye, divine ear.",
87: "'Fifth, second and sixth' is copied from Xuanzang's order, which differs from the translations' order of the six powers.",
88: "'When' should be 'because': these knowledges arise only in the continuum of one beyond training.",
89: "LS's gloss of 'the others' as listeners and pratyekabuddhas is inaccurate: ordinary beings can also have this kind of movement.",
90: "除彼同類 means 'except one of the same kind', i.e. an intermediate being sees intermediate beings; otherwise only the very pure cultivated divine eye sees them.",
91: "Pruden slip: the intermediate dhyāna is with vicāra and without vitarka. LS corrects it.",
92: "The direction is reversed: the case is dying in the second dhyāna or above and being reborn in the Brahmā world.",
93: "'Absorption' / 'meditative attainment' is wrong: 'innately pure' (生得淨) means a non-meditative mind — one cannot die in meditation — and includes unobscured-indeterminate minds.",
94: "Several problems: pure attainments include the immaterial ones; 'pure' includes unobscured-indeterminate minds; an innate wholesome mind is not meditative even in a meditative stage; and LS turns LVP's 'fine-material realm' into the Brahmā world.",
95: "LS's '[defiled thought of]' is superfluous: no mind of a higher stage arises at all, defiled or pure.",
96: "Here Pruden's 'pure' means with-outflow (śuddhaka), the opposite of his usual 'pure' for outflow-free.",
97: "'Duration-and-change' should be just 'change': when 生住異滅 expresses the three characteristics, 住異 is the characteristic of change.",
98: "LS's added 'and the intermediate meditation' is superfluous: the eleven stages already include it.",
99: "'Produce' should be 'born': those born in the third and fourth dhyānas lack the immeasurable of joy; possessing and manifesting are different.",
100: "Pruden's 'third' should be 'fourth', and LS's 'formless meditative attainment' should be the immaterial realm (a non-meditative state). Innate wholesome is not 'produced by retribution': retribution is indeterminate.",
}

